    "q-161548": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161548",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the type II region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,-3\\le x\\le 5{y}^{4}+2\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is some finite region in the $yz$-plane. Then,\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {u}_{2}(y,z)\\}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14216491/assets/1b49704bf6d5837a18d8b5dbb8b420d430d5e3881507540fb62a45cf1f6ce52f.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(y,z)=-3\\,{u}_{2}(y,z)=5{y}^{4}+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-3}^{5{y}^{4}+2}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
