    "q-161564": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161564",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz\n$$\n\nis defined over the type II region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-z\\le y\\le 1,\\,4\\le z\\le 5,\\,-\\sqrt{y+z}\\le x\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b?$",
      "worked_solution": "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {u}_{2}(y,z)\\}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14216491/assets/a905e1f60fb0aa865c071fcbccbfecb30dcac7a74b4b647d24a40f4c89ff1935.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(y,z)=-\\sqrt{y+z},\\,{u}_{2}(y,z)=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y+z}}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-z\\le y\\le 1,\\,4\\le z\\le 5\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y+z}}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA & ={\\int }_{4}^{5}{\\int }_{-z}^{1}[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y+z}}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dy\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{5}{\\int }_{-z}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y+z}}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=4+5=9.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
