      "source_example_id": "e-11348",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region",
      "kp_id": "e-11348"
    },
    "q-161558": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161558",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14216491/assets/5780b9e67272fdd78a24aab108fb9c672ee76ff2d62376991cea03c7efaa81fe.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{12}\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14216491/assets/240cc6d74e266579aa848be820fd0014672aa0654f079c079472a896ad44c5fe.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-y+2z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14216491/assets/27862dcc3effe5801c812f5c8944c0134d13bb38f99d337b0a4e20e9ff815639.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-y+2z\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{12}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y+2z}\\frac{z}{12}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y+2z}\\frac{z}{12}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-y+2z}\\frac{z}{12}\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{z}{12}[x]_{x=0}^{x=4-y+2z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{z}{12}(4-y+2z)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{12}(4z-yz+2{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{12}(4z-yz+2{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{12}(4z-yz+2{z}^{2})\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{12}{[2{z}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y{z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{3}]}_{z=0}^{z=3}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{12}(18-\\frac{9}{2}y+18)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(3-\\frac{3}{8}y)\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(3-\\frac{3}{8}y)\\,dy & ={[3y-\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =(12-3)-0 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
