      ],
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14216491/observations/step-q161558-f6d07f0708231e9f1840.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14216491/observations/step-q161558-f6d07f0708231e9f1840.png",
      "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/tasks/14216491/topics/2141/lesson",
      "captured_at": 1792015487.798964,
      "math_academy_id": "q-161558",
      "topic_id": "2141",
      "sequence_position": null,
      "source_step": {
        "dom_id": "step-q161558",
        "placement_id": null,
        "content_id": null,
        "type": null
      },
      "source_example_id": "e-12369",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region Given a Diagram",
      "kp_id": "e-12369"
    },
    "q-161783": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161783",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14216491/assets/3fe3ec24f8f8006f22bde8ba2dc39fd3cd3f73668b06f8ea4a74e4ed5206a193.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{10-2y-z}\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14216491/assets/2a20f2651c24e48871028b44fe61da4600d632481877ef32ccdfd3241262a53b.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 5-y-\\frac{z}{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14216491/assets/1ba7c02f254313a73a813e4e43c44c25523ce77dd47e594a1db3b412a6bd5c3d.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 5-y-\\frac{z}{2}\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{10-2y-z}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{5-y-z/2}\\frac{1}{10-2y-z}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{5-y-z/2}\\frac{1}{10-2y-z}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{5-y-z/2}\\frac{1}{10-2y-z}\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{10-2y-z}[x]_{x=0}^{x=5-y-z/2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{10-2y-z}(5-y-\\frac{z}{2})\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{z}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=4}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2\\,dy & =[2y]_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =6-0 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
